4. Proposition 1 (phases) -- recomputation and the base question
   3n+1 from 2^60-1       N =  310 odd iterates: D_N{log_2 m} = 0.0126, D_N{pure rotation log_2 n + l log_2 q} = 0.0053, 1/sqrt(N) = 0.0568, D_N{log_10 m} = 0.0395
        shift log2 C_l: at l=N/2: 0.00000, at l=0.9N: 0.07159, last: 0.24659; max-min over last half: 2.47e-01; accumulated in the last 20 odd steps: 0.15609; identity vs product: 8.7e-14
   3n+1 from 2^200-1      N =  980 odd iterates: D_N{log_2 m} = 0.0046, D_N{pure rotation log_2 n + l log_2 q} = 0.0027, 1/sqrt(N) = 0.0319, D_N{log_10 m} = 0.0233
        shift log2 C_l: at l=N/2: 0.00000, at l=0.9N: 0.00000, last: 0.32171; max-min over last half: 3.22e-01; accumulated in the last 20 odd steps: 0.32061; identity vs product: 3.1e-13
   3n+1 record 63728127   N =  358 odd iterates: D_N{log_2 m} = 0.0063, D_N{pure rotation log_2 n + l log_2 q} = 0.0038, 1/sqrt(N) = 0.0529, D_N{log_10 m} = 0.0331
        shift log2 C_l: at l=N/2: 0.00000, at l=0.9N: 0.00090, last: 0.24296; max-min over last half: 2.43e-01; accumulated in the last 20 odd steps: 0.24141; identity vs product: 9.4e-14
   5n+1 from 7            N = 3001 odd iterates: D_N{log_2 m} = 0.0016, D_N{pure rotation log_2 n + l log_2 q} = 0.0011, 1/sqrt(N) = 0.0183, D_N{log_10 m} = 0.0151
        shift log2 C_l: at l=N/2: 0.10258, at l=0.9N: 0.10258, last: 0.10258; max-min over last half: 9.09e-13; accumulated in the last 20 odd steps: 0.00000; identity vs product: 1.1e-12
   reading: for the finite 3n+1 orbits the 'convergence' of the shift is finiteness: it is ~0 while the iterates are huge and accumulates
   its whole value in the last few dozen odd steps (small m_j); only the (presumed divergent) 5n+1 orbit shows genuine convergence (1e-12).
   one-line reason for 'Weyl + convergent = equidistributed': for h != 0, |e(h(x_l+y_l)) - e(h(x_l+y))| <= 2 pi |h| |y_l - y| -> 0,
   so the Weyl sums of x_l + y_l and of the shifted rotation x_l + y have the same Cesaro limit 0.  CONFIRMED (base 2).
   Base question: log_2 m_l = log_2 Q_l + l alpha - d_l with d_l an INTEGER, so {log_2 m_l} = {log_2 Q_l + l alpha} (pure rotation + convergent).
   In base 10: log_10 m_l = log_10 Q_l + l log_10 3 - d_l log_10 2, and d_l log_10 2 is NOT an integer: the phase is the linear form
   l log_10 3 - d_l log_10 2 along the orbit's own path (l, d_l); no rotation argument applies. Synthetic demonstration (a word with v_l in {1,2}
   chosen greedily, m_l := 3^l 2^(-d_l), exactly the identity's shape with Q constant):
      N = 5000: D_N{log_2 m} = 0.0014 (equidistributed), D_N{log_10 m} = 0.6500, base-10 phases confined to [0.000, 0.350]
   So base-2 significand equidistribution (what Prop. 1 proves) does not imply base-10 Benford; whether an actual divergent orbit can
   choose its valuations like this is unknown -- the note's proof does not address it. 'Benford's law' must be read as 'in base 2'. GAP.
   Attribution: Kontorovich-Miller (2005) and Lagarias-Soundararajan (2006) prove base-B Benford for the first N iterates of MOST seeds
   (unconditional, finite segments, the valuations acting as a random walk on the circle); Prop. 1 is a conditional statement about ONE
   non-eventually-periodic orbit (vacuous for 3n+1 if Collatz holds) in base 2 only, where the valuations drop out. Related but not the same content.
====================================================================================================
5. Proposition 2 (the two-place clock Q_l)
   n=    27 ( 41 odd steps): identity 2^d_l m_l = 3^l n + S_(l-1): True; Q_l = n C_l: True; Q_(l+1)-Q_l = Q_l/(3 m_l): True; Q_l increasing: True;
        |Q_l|_2 = 2^-d_l and |Q_l|_3 = 3^l (i.e. 3 does not divide m_l) for 1 <= l <= 41: True; at l = 0, |Q_0|_3 = |n|_3 = 3^-3
        sum_(j<L) 1/m_j = 0.551743; log(Q_L/n) = 0.181362; ratio = 3.0422 (note claims ratio <= 3; correct: 3 < ratio <= 3 + 1/min m_j = 3.2000)
   n=   703 ( 62 odd steps): identity 2^d_l m_l = 3^l n + S_(l-1): True; Q_l = n C_l: True; Q_(l+1)-Q_l = Q_l/(3 m_l): True; Q_l increasing: True;
        |Q_l|_2 = 2^-d_l and |Q_l|_3 = 3^l (i.e. 3 does not divide m_l) for 1 <= l <= 62: True; at l = 0, |Q_0|_3 = |n|_3 = 3^-0
        sum_(j<L) 1/m_j = 0.581786; log(Q_L/n) = 0.190577; ratio = 3.0528 (note claims ratio <= 3; correct: 3 < ratio <= 3 + 1/min m_j = 3.2000)
   n= 26623 (113 odd steps): identity 2^d_l m_l = 3^l n + S_(l-1): True; Q_l = n C_l: True; Q_(l+1)-Q_l = Q_l/(3 m_l): True; Q_l increasing: True;
        |Q_l|_2 = 2^-d_l and |Q_l|_3 = 3^l (i.e. 3 does not divide m_l) for 1 <= l <= 113: True; at l = 0, |Q_0|_3 = |n|_3 = 3^-0
        sum_(j<L) 1/m_j = 0.420534; log(Q_L/n) = 0.137834; ratio = 3.0510 (note claims ratio <= 3; correct: 3 < ratio <= 3 + 1/min m_j = 3.2000)
   over all odd n <= 4000 (full orbits to 1): sum 1/m_j / log(Q_L/n) in [3.0004, 3.1391]  -- always > 3 (log(1+x) < x), never <= 3
   correct inequality: x/(1+x) <= log(1+x) <= x with x = 1/(3 m) <= 1/3 gives  3 log(Q_inf/n) <= sum 1/m_l <= (3 + 1/min_l m_l) log(Q_inf/n) <= 4 log(Q_inf/n).
   The note's 'sum 1/m_l <= 3 log(Q_inf/n)' has the inequality reversed; the universal constant in the stated direction is 4. ERROR (constant), harmless for the use made of it.
   3 | m_l for some l >= 1 on the orbit of odd n <= 10^5: 0 orbits (algebra: m_l = 2^-v (3 m_(l-1) + 1) = 2^-v mod 3 != 0)
